that it just holds on to everything you tell it, kind of like a human brain would.

19
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.040
But the truth? The context window is just a limited working input space for a single request.

20
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.760
It is not durable memory. Think about the difference between a brain and a physical desk.

21
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.440
If you pile way too many documents onto a desk, one of two things happens.

22
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.480
Either it completely overflows and stuff falls off the edge,

23
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.760
or you get what's called the lost in the middle effect. That's where important

24
00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:53.520
documents just get buried under a mountain of other papers, and they're recalled far less

25
00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:58.640
reliably than whatever's sitting right on top. And this brilliantly illustrates the reality of

26
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.400
context management. If a piece of context isn't actively placed on that desk for a specific request

27
00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.280
or written down somewhere else permanently, it's simply gone. You literally have to manage

28
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.840
this budget actively. You can't just assume the AI remembers all your past prompts.

