00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:29.680
context protocol, or MCP, cleanly forces us to distinguish exactly how our AI interacts with

46
00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:34.400
the outside world. If you want the model to actually execute a task, you build a tool.

47
00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.400
But if you just need the model to read a catalog or maybe a policy document,

48
00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:43.040
you use a resource, which provides safe, read-only context. And if you're packaging

49
00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.160
reusable instructions, well, you use a prompt. Mixing these up, like giving a model an active

50
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.880
tool when it really just needed to read a static resource, adds so much unnecessary risk and

51
00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.320
complexity to your system. And when we design schemas for these interactions,

52
00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:00.640
we absolutely have to consider missing data. Enter the nullable field.

53
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.480
Nullable fields are not just some minor formatting detail. They are a crucial

54
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:09.280
anti-fabrication tool. Let's say you require an AI to extract an invoice total, but the document

55
00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:14.160
doesn't actually have one. A strict requirement will force the AI to hallucinate a guess just

56
00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.720
to fill the box. By making the field nullable, you give the system a graceful way out to say,

57
00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.720
hey, this fact is genuinely missing, which allows you to route it safely to a human for review.

